1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,000
A Boolean function takes n bits in and returns one bit out; its truth table has two to the n rows, and that table is the whole function.

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:15,000
Three variables already give 256 different functions, because each of the eight rows can independently be a zero or a one.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:22,000
A Boolean expression describes one column: variables, AND, OR and NOT, combined into a formula that is evaluated row by row.

4
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:30,000
Minimisation means the same column described with fewer terms and fewer literals: fewer gates, less delay, less power.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:38,000
Every identity is a claim about a truth table: both sides agree on every row. Nothing is proved by rearranging symbols alone.

6
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:45,000
Duality: swap AND with OR and swap zero with one in a true identity, and the result is also true.

7
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:52,000
x plus x is x, and x times x is x. There is no coefficient two here, and that is where ordinary algebra stops helping.

8
00:00:52,000 --> 00:01:00,000
De Morgan: the complement of a product is the sum of the complements, and the complement of a sum is the product of the complements.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:08,000
Absorption: x plus x times y is x. A term that is contained in another term adds nothing.

10
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:15,000
Consensus: x y plus x-prime z plus y z is x y plus x-prime z. The third term is already covered by the first two.

11
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:22,000
Worked: f equals a-prime c plus a c-prime plus b-prime c has minterms one, three, four, five and six.

12
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:30,000
But algebra alone cannot promise a minimum: a different order of steps reaches a different expression, and nothing tells you which is smallest.

13
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:38,000
A minterm is one row written as a product: every variable appears once, plain where the row has a one and complemented where it has a zero.

14
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:45,000
A maxterm is one row written as a sum, and maxterm i is the complement of minterm i.

15
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:52,000
Canonical sum of products ORs the minterms where the function is one; canonical product of sums ANDs the maxterms where it is zero.

16
00:01:52,000 --> 00:02:00,000
Both canonical forms always exist and both are unique, and neither is minimal: every term carries every variable.

17
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:08,000
The map is the truth table bent into a rectangle so that rows differing in one bit are placed next to each other.

18
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:15,000
That is why the labels run 00, 01, 11, 10. Consecutive labels differ in one bit, which binary counting order would not give.

19
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:22,000
The edges wrap: the left column touches the right column, and the top row touches the bottom, because the map is a cylinder and then a torus.

20
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:30,000
A block of two to the k cells drops k variables, so a pair keeps n minus one literals and a quad keeps n minus two.

21
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:38,000
The rules: cover every one, take the largest blocks first, overlapping is allowed, wrapping is allowed, and only one, two, four, eight or sixteen cells.

22
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:45,000
A prime implicant is a block that cannot be made larger. GATE 2015 Set 3 Q43 asks only for their number, for f equals sigma of zero, two, four, five, six, ten.

23
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:52,000
There are three: the quad w-prime z-prime over zero, two, four and six; the pair w-prime x y-prime over four and five; the pair x-prime y z-prime over two and ten.

24
00:02:52,000 --> 00:03:00,000
A pair sitting inside that quad, such as zero with two, is not prime, because a larger block already contains it.

25
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:08,000
A don't-care is a row the specification never uses. Its output may be zero or one, whichever helps the grouping.

26
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:15,000
GATE 2018 Q49: F of P, Q, R, S is sigma m of zero, two, five, seven, nine, eleven, plus d of three, eight, ten, twelve, fourteen.

27
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:23,000
Five prime implicants exist, and three of them cover a one that no other prime implicant covers: Q-prime S-prime, P-prime Q S, and P Q-prime.

28
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:30,000
Essential means it covers a one that no other prime implicant covers. A don't-care can never make an implicant essential.

29
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:38,000
For a product of sums, group the zeros instead. Each block of zeros becomes one sum term, with every literal flipped.

30
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:45,000
GATE 2017 Set 2 Q28: f is sigma m of zero, one, two, three, seven, eight, ten plus d of five, six, eleven, fifteen. The zeros are four, nine, twelve, thirteen, fourteen.

31
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:53,000
Four, six, twelve and fourteen group to x z-bar; nine, eleven, thirteen and fifteen group to w z. So f-bar is x z-bar plus w z.

32
00:03:53,000 --> 00:04:00,000
That is option A. The others fail on a row where f is one: w-bar plus z times x plus z is zero at minterm zero, for example.

33
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:08,000
The tabular method is the same search on paper, with no picture: write every minterm in binary and group them by the number of ones.

34
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:15,000
Combine any two terms that differ in exactly one bit and mark that bit with a dash. Repeat until nothing combines; what remains is the list of prime implicants.

35
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:22,000
For f equals sigma of zero, two, four, five, six, ten: the four minterms zero, two, four, six collapse to zero dash dash zero, and two pairs survive. Three prime implicants, the same three the map gave.

36
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:30,000
Then the cover chart: a minterm covered by a single prime implicant makes that implicant essential. When no column has a single cross, the map is cyclic and you must choose.

37
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:38,000
Procedure: write the canonical form, draw the map or build the table, take the largest blocks, then check every one is covered and no zero is.

38
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:45,000
Trap one: a don't-care is not a one. Using one is optional, and a block may never contain a zero.

39
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:53,000
Trap two: prime implicants are not the answer; a cover is. Trap three: fewest terms is not always fewest literals.

40
00:04:53,000 --> 00:05:00,000
Trap four: grouping zeros gives a product of sums, and every literal flips. Check two rows by hand before you trust the answer.

